{"name":"Bridesmaid Sash","hex":"#f6dfd8","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bridesmaid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A woman who attends a bride during her wedding ceremony, as part of the main wedding party.","A person or team that perennially finishes well, but never first."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To act as a bridesmaid for; to attend a bride during her wedding ceremony."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bridesmaid"},{"term":"sash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of cloth designed to be worn around the waist.","A decorative length of cloth worn over the shoulder to the opposite hip, often for ceremonial or other formal occasions."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To adorn with a sash."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sash"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bridesmaid","blocks":[{"text":"Bridesmaids are members of the bride's party at Western wedding ceremonies. A bridesmaid is typically a young woman and often the bride's close friend or relative. She attends to the bride on the day of a wedding or marriage ceremony. Traditionally, bridesmaids were chosen from unwed young women of marriageable age."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridesmaid","wikidata":"Q902435"},{"title":"Sash","blocks":[{"text":"A sash is a large and usually colorful ribbon or band of material worn around the human body, either draping from one shoulder to the opposing hip and back up, or else encircling the waist. The sash around the waist may be worn in daily attire, but the sash from shoulder to hip is typically only worn on ceremonial occasions. Ceremonial sashes are also found in a V-shaped format, draping straight from both shoulders down, intersecting and forming an angle over the chest or abdomen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg/330px-Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sash","wikidata":"Q1370565"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Lehnga.jpg/500px-Lehnga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Lehnga.jpg/500px-Lehnga.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Four bridesmaids wearing gagra cholis, the traditional dress of North India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALehnga.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Bridesmaid_and_junior_bridesmaid-crop.jpg/330px-Bridesmaid_and_junior_bridesmaid-crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Bridesmaid_and_junior_bridesmaid-crop.jpg/330px-Bridesmaid_and_junior_bridesmaid-crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Junior bridesmaid in North Carolina, dressed in white like the bride.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABridesmaid_and_junior_bridesmaid-crop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Bridesmaids1929.jpg/500px-Bridesmaids1929.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Bridesmaids1929.jpg/1280px-Bridesmaids1929.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Bridesmaids1929.jpg/500px-Bridesmaids1929.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The bride (center) with her maid of honor (third from left) and bridesmaids. The maid of honor can be distinguished by her dress, which differs somewhat from that of the other bridesmaids, as well as by her position in the traditional place of honor at the immediate right of the principal party. From 1929, in Minnesota, US.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABridesmaids1929.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg/500px-Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg/1280px-Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg/500px-Philip_V_of_Spain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Philip V, King of Spain wearing two types of sash: a red waist sash and the blue sash (Cordon Bleu) of the Order of the Holy Spirit","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APhilip_V_of_Spain.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg/500px-1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg/1280px-1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg/500px-1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bonaparte at the Pont d'Arcole, a 1796 portrait of Napoleon by Antoine-Jean Gros depicting him wearing a sash","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1801_Antoine-Jean_Gros_-_Bonaparte_on_the_Bridge_at_Arcole.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/George_Wa